# Line endings are part of this repository's contents, not a local preference.
#
# ⚠️ Several tests read the project's own source and assert on it — that a tool carries a given
# instruction, that the terminal delegates its budget to the shared rule — and some of those
# assertions span a line break. On a Windows checkout with the default `core.autocrlf=true`, git
# rewrote every LF to CRLF on the way out, so a regex written as `…you " +\n\s+"report here` could
# not match a file that now held `\r\n`. CI went red on the Windows job alone, for weeks, on code
# that was correct — the checkout had changed the bytes under the test.
#
# `eol=lf` is stronger than a preference: it overrides `core.autocrlf` for everyone, so the working
# tree holds what the repository holds on every platform. `tests/sourceBytes.test.ts` guards the
# file so this cannot be dropped again without a test saying so.
#
# This says nothing about files the *user* edits: those arrive with whatever endings they have, and
# `src/core/text/findText.ts` is what makes `edit_file` match them either way.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never translated, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.svg text eol=lf
